From 69c969c52ea3d8701d703e106182e08ee4b6a325 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tom Proctor <tomhjp@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 20:03:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Render 24-bit colors as inline styles

v3.17.0 started to parse 24-bit SGR colors (38;2;r;g;b and 48;2;r;g;b)
into the style, but asClasses drops them, so the text shows in the
default colors. Programs that detect COLORTERM=truecolor, such as
coding agents and many modern CLIs, send all of their colors this way.

There can't be a CSS class for each 24-bit color, so write them as an
inline style attribute on the span, next to the classes for the other
attributes:

    <span class="term-fg1" style="color:#0000ff;">bold blue</span>

8-bit and 16-color codes still render as classes only, so output that
doesn't use 24-bit colors is unchanged.

Pages that use a Content-Security-Policy without 'unsafe-inline' in
style-src will ignore the style attributes, and the text falls back to
the default colors as before.
